==English==
[[Image:Sachertorte_DSC03027.JPG|thumb|right|A slice of cake (1), specifically a slice of a [[torte]].]]
[[File:Pound layer cake.jpg|right|thumb|A layer cake from which a slice has been removed.]]

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=dessert|inh|enm>cake>dessert|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|cake}}, from {{der|en|non|kaka||cake}} (compare {{cog|no|kake}}, Icelandic/Swedish {{m|sv|kaka}}, Danish {{m|da|kage}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kakǭ}}, of disputed origin. Likely a distant cognate with {{m|en|kaak}}. Perhaps related to {{m|en|cookie}}, {{m|en|kuchen}}, and {{m|en|quiche}}. {{doublet|en|coca|pos1=pastry}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|kāk}}, {{IPA|en|/keɪk/|[ˈk̟ʰeɪ̯k̚]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-cake.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-cake.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q13276}}A [[rich]], [[sweet]] dessert [[food]], typically made of [[flour]], [[sugar]]{{,}} and [[eggs]] and baked in an [[oven]], and often covered in [[icing]].<!--encyclopedic info, and definitions of other words! A [[gâteau]] or sponge cake includes a chemical leavening agent (e.g. baking powder), making it light and airy. The denser [[torte]] omits the leavening agent, and uses less flour and more eggs.-->
#: {{syn|en|gateau}}
# A small mass of baked [[dough]], especially a thin [[loaf]] from [[unleavened]] dough.
#: {{co|en|an oatmeal '''cake'''}}
#: {{co|en|a johnny'''cake'''}}
# A thin [[wafer]]-shaped mass of fried [[batter]]; a [[griddlecake]] or [[pancake]].
#: {{co|en|buckwheat '''cakes'''}}
# A [[block]] of any various [[dense]] materials.
#: {{syn|en|block}}
#: {{co|en|a '''cake''' of soap}}
#: {{co|en|a '''cake''' of sand}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|1|passage='''Cakes''' of rustling ice come rolling down the flood.}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[trivial]]ly easy [[task]] or [[responsibility]]; from '''a [[piece of cake]]'''.
#: {{syn|en|piece of cake|Thesaurus:easy thing}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} [[money|Money]].
# {{n-g|Used to describe the doctrine of [[have one's cake and eat it too|having one's '''cake''' and eating it too]].}}
#* '''2018''', The Guardian, "UK's aspirations for post-Brexit trade deal an illusion, says Donald Tusk",  Daniel Boffey, Peter Walker, Jennifer Rankin, and Heather Stewart, 23 February 2018
#*: "It looks like the '''cake''' [and eat it] philosophy is still alive." Quote attributed to [[wikipedia:Donald Tusk|Donald Tusk]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[buttock]], especially one that is exceptionally [[plump]].
#: {{ux|en|Mmm, I'd like to cut me some of that '''cake'''!}}
# {{lb|en|pyrotechnics}} A [[multi-]][[shot]] [[fireworks]] [[assembly]] comprising several [[tube]]s, each with a fireworks [[effect]], lit by a single [[fuse]].

